## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design an incremental data structure for computing a non-trivial graph statistic under streaming batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental degree-based metrics
- Amortized analysis for batch/streaming updates
- Space-time trade-offs in dynamic graph algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
let deg = Array(n).fill(0);
let P = 0;

function addEdge(u, v) {
  const du = deg[u], dv = deg[v];
  P += 2 * (du + dv);
  deg[u] = du + 1;
  deg[v] = dv + 1;
}

function processBatch(edges) {
  edges.forEach(([u, v]) => addEdge(u, v));
  return P;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge deletions?
- How does multi-graph handling (parallel edges) affect this approach?
- What are the time and space complexities compared to recomputing from scratch?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?
